add(1998,8,5.5,'The common-neighbour rules impose a precise local graph structure and degree equation; ruling out degree six requires a second neighbourhood argument.','Recovering the size of a triangle-free trade network',r'A town network has between 12 and 28 vertices. Adjacent vertices have no common neighbour, while nonadjacent vertices have exactly two common neighbours. Determine the number of vertices.',r'Fix a vertex $X$ with neighbours $T_1,\ldots,T_m$. These neighbours are independent. Every pair $T_i,T_j$ has exactly one common neighbour other than $X$; every non-neighbour of $X$ is adjacent to exactly two $T_i$. Thus these non-neighbours are in bijection with pairs $\{i,j\}$, denoted $N_{ij}$, and the order is $1+m+\binom m2$. The range gives $m=5$ or 6, hence order 16 or 22. The same argument at any vertex shows regularity. If $m=6$, the other four neighbours of $N_{12}$ must be among the six $N_{ij}$ with $i,j\in\{3,4,5,6\}$. Choose one non-neighbour, say $N_{34}$. Its only possible common neighbour with $N_{12}$ is $N_{56}$, contradicting the required two. Therefore the order is 16. Existence is verified by joining $X$ to all $T_i$, each $N_{ij}$ to $T_i,T_j$, and two $N$-vertices exactly when their index pairs are disjoint, for indices 1 through 5.','Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Double counting','Common neighbours','Extremal arguments'],source=s)
