add(2001,8,5,'Every out-neighbourhood must contain a directed cycle, forcing outdegree at least three; a seven-vertex cyclic tournament attains the bound.','A smallest flight network with two-step alternatives',r'A finite directed graph has no pair of opposite arcs and each vertex has an outgoing arc. Every arc $A\to C$ also admits a two-step path $A\to B\to C$. Find the smallest possible number of vertices.',r'The answer is 7. Fix a vertex $A$. For every out-neighbour $C$, the two-step condition gives another out-neighbour $B$ with $B\to C$. Hence the graph induced by the out-neighbours has positive indegree at every vertex. It contains a directed cycle, whose length is at least three because loops and opposite arcs are unavailable. Thus every vertex has outdegree at least three. If there are $n$ vertices, $3n\le n(n-1)/2$, so $n\ge7$. For attainment use vertices modulo 7, with arcs from $x$ to $x+1,x+2,x+4$. There are no opposite arcs, and each allowed difference is twice another allowed difference modulo 7: $1=4+4$, $2=1+1$, $4=2+2$. These identities supply all required two-step paths.','Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Directed cycles','Double counting','Constructions'],source=s)
