add(2004,3,4,'Each residue chain modulo the fixed displacement must be paired by adjacent swaps, so the displacement is a divisor of 1002 and each choice is unique.','Permutations with equal displacement at every position',r'How many permutations $a_1,\ldots,a_{2004}$ of $1,\ldots,2004$ satisfy $|a_i-i|=d>0$ for every position $i$?',r'For fixed $d$, view each residue class modulo $d$ as an increasing path of available values. The smallest vertex can only map to the next one. Conversely that next vertex must map back, since the smallest value has no other possible preimage. Removing the pair and repeating shows every path must have even length, with its adjacent vertices swapped uniquely. Writing $2004=qd+r$ with $0\le r<d$, the paths have lengths $q$ or $q+1$. Both cannot occur, so $r=0$ and $q$ is even. Thus exactly the positive divisors $d$ of 1002 work. Since $1002=2\cdot3\cdot167$, there are 8.','Combinatorics',['Permutations','Graph theory','Parity','Divisors'],source=s)
add(2004,4,3.5,'Coordinates in a unit equilateral triangle give BF perpendicular to FG and BF²=3FG², hence angles 30,90,60 at B,F,G.',source=s)
