add(2004,7,4.5,'Even-position partial sums form a simple cycle on the integer line with jumps at most two; such a cycle has exactly two odd jumps, accounting for the red beads.','Red beads in an indivisible balanced necklace',r'A necklace has an even number $n\ge4$ of red, blue and green beads, with equally many blue and green. It cannot be split into two strings of positive even lengths, each containing equally many blue and green beads. Determine the possible number of red beads.',r'There must be exactly two red beads. Give blue weight 1, green weight −1 and red weight 0. Take partial sums after every two beads. No proper even-length zero-sum interval is allowed, so these partial sums are distinct except for the final return to zero. Consecutive values differ by 1 or 2 in absolute value; a zero difference would already be forbidden. For a cycle with at least three vertices, exactly two edges have odd length. To prove this, its smallest vertex has neighbours exactly one and two units larger; remove it and replace its two incident edges by the edge between these neighbours. The number of odd edges is unchanged, and induction reduces to a triangle. In the two-edge case there are either two odd edges or none; the latter would give no red beads, impossible because some adjacent blue and green pair would form a forbidden balanced string. A two-bead block has odd weight exactly when it contains one red bead; it cannot contain two reds. Hence the red count is two. This is attainable with equal positive runs of blue and green separated by the two reds: every even balanced proper interval would need both separating reds and then either no or all of each run, which is impossible.','Combinatorics',['Invariants','Partial sums','Parity','Graph theory','Necklaces'],source=s)
